# UpgradeAtlas — graphite product direction

Status: graphite theme and smooth scrolling/motion selected by Eli. This consolidated direction is the final grilling restatement, pending overall confirmation before destination implementation. It supersedes the light/silver proposal in `design-research/DIRECTION_DRAFT.md` and `PARENT_REVIEW.md`. The reference board remains historical inspiration, not the selected application palette.

## Product and visual intent

Build the specified laptop buying, comparison and upgrade advisor, not a standalone calculator or generic SaaS landing. The main experience uses consistent graphite/charcoal surfaces, pale typography, finely controlled borders and one restrained cool accent. Strong physical laptop imagery is the expressive focal point. No switch back to a light-first identity; secondary dark surfaces provide hierarchy without decorative card piles.

The two supplied screenshots are partial inspiration, not exact targets. From the Hirefy screenshot, borrow depth, calm composition and controlled edge illumination; do not copy its globe, hands, team-hiring copy or numeric claim. From the Deblot screenshot, borrow graphite discipline, confident technical hierarchy and structural alignment; do not copy its ASCII head, AI-platform content, typography wholesale or numbered filler sections. A still image is not evidence of animation.

Arabic-first RTL receives deliberate font, spacing and optical alignment; English LTR is a first-class counterpart. Exact model numbers and monetary amounts retain readable directional isolation. Fonts, icons and hardware media require appropriate rights; reference screenshots are not application assets.

## Information architecture

- Buyer: home -> finder/catalog -> exact-SKU details -> compare 2–4 configurations -> recorded retailer offers when supported by evidence.
- Owner: identify exact SKU -> documented upgrade layout and uncertainty -> optional cost-only upgrade-or-replace calculator.
- Trust/editorial: source context, verification dates and unknown/conflict states, plus the already-required publishing/admin workflow. Do not invent real data to fill a polished layout.

The calculator becomes a supporting owner tool. It is not the hero, navigation model or entire product. The redesign must preserve its verified integer-piaster calculations and request-state protections.

## Technology and motion choices

Keep React + JavaScript/JSX with semantic HTML5, CSS3/Tailwind and Vite. Keep PHP 8.3/Laravel 13 REST APIs and PostgreSQL 16; Git/GitHub/Docker remain the development tools. No Next.js or Python/FastAPI application runtime. Prefer free/open-source and existing platform capabilities over additional services.

Use Lenis for responsive desktop scroll smoothing when appropriate, with native touch behavior, correct lifecycle cleanup and no duplicate animation loop. Parent inspected the official Lenis repository README and MIT license; `design-research/lenis-source-check.json` binds the source observations. This is source research, not an integration test.

Use native CSS/IntersectionObserver/Web Animations for purposeful product entrances, section reveals, navigation/selection feedback and comparison-tray transitions. Introduce a heavier animation system only if an actual approved sequence requires it; do not stack libraries by default. Proposed taste dials: DESIGN_VARIANCE 6, MOTION_INTENSITY 5, VISUAL_DENSITY 4 on discovery surfaces, with denser comparison information only where needed.

Do not hijack scroll with long pinned slides, delay usable content until animation finishes, break keyboard focus or force smoothing on touch input. Reduced-motion preference must disable nonessential movement and use ordinary scrolling. All motion requires cleanup and verification on mobile and desktop.

## Implementation acceptance boundary

The first visual proof must establish the site identity on the home, catalog and comparison surfaces together, not present another isolated form as a whole website. Browser-visible states, actual interactions and responsive Arabic/English views are required; screenshots and tests must match the implementation that exists. No dead CTAs or fabricated availability, specification, compatibility, benchmark or partnership claims.

Full schema bootstrap/parity, source-backed publication and existing draft-exposure issues remain independent open gates. A visual redesign does not resolve them. No database reset, destructive migration, paid dependency, public upload or deployment is authorized by this design discussion.
